<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wadi Araba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arabah is the long desert valley running south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>, now forming part of the border between Israel and Jordan. Rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ores at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> and Timna, it was an important mining area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Ages</a>, and the land of Edom lay in the mountains on its eastern side. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">Caravan routes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> also crossed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabah.jpg" alt="Arabah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: בתיה בן צבי · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PikiWiki_Israel_8801_Plants_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
